 Crossings take `type mcb`, `mcb_od`, `ahb`, `uwc` or `open`. A trap catches a
 movement running the way it faces.
 
+## Temporary speed restrictions
+
+```sbx
+node A boundary
+node B boundary
+edge E1 from A to B length 1200 speed 90 direction down
+section TA over E1
+
+restriction TSR1 on E1 at 400 for 500 speed 20 reason "renewals"
+```
+
+A restriction starts where it says and runs the length it says, over as many
+pieces of track as that takes, following the road the points lie for. `at` is
+metres from the start of the named edge, `for` is how long the slack is, and
+`speed` is the mph a train may do inside it. Anything written after the speed is
+kept as an attribute, so a slack can carry the reason it is there. Where two
+restrictions cover the same metre of track the slower one governs, and an
+extent that runs out of track stops there.
+
+Both ends are marks on the ground rather than boundaries of a range: a slack
+written `at 400 for 500` covers the 400 metre mark, the 900 metre mark, and
+everything in between. Two of them may meet at a mark without a metre of line
+speed between them.
+
+Trains have to be brought down to it. The braking run is worked back from the
+first metre of the restriction, from the line speed of the track it sits on,
+using the scheme's braking rate and reaction time and the gradients a train
+meets on the way, and the `restriction-room` rule reports a slack the scheme
+has no room to brake for.
+
+The rest of the toolkit reads the restricted speeds rather than the line speed
+wherever the answer depends on how fast a train is going. Signal spacing is
+checked at the fastest speed a train may be doing over a route, which only
+drops where a slack covers the whole of a piece of track. Headway is worked at
+the slowest speed anywhere in the block, since one slow stretch decides the run
+time over all of it. A driver in a simulation keeps to whatever is permitted
+where the front of the train is standing.
+
